### 3.2 Conventions

1. All informational continuity components \(C_i\) are dimensionless, bounded in \([0,1]\), and normalized against the biological baseline at \(t_d^-\).
2. The value \(C_i = 1\) denotes identity-preserving persistence relative to \(D_B\). The value \(C_i = 0\) denotes total discontinuity.
3. The latent space \(\mathcal{L}\) admits no third-person metric. Elements of \(\mathcal{L}\) are denoted by \(\Phi\), \(C_\Phi\), or \(\phi\) with subscripts. No arithmetic is performed on \(\mathcal{L}\) directly.
4. The credence operator \(\Omega : \mathcal{H} \to [0,1]\) is the only bridge from \(\mathcal{L}\) to numerical reasoning. It is not a measurement; it is a bounded rational credence.
5. Divergence \(\mathcal{D}\) is symmetric: \(\mathcal{D}(X,Y,t) = \mathcal{D}(Y,X,t)\). It satisfies the triangle inequality when the sub-metrics \(d_i\) are proper metrics.
6. All probabilities are in \([0,1]\). All logarithmic expressions use natural log unless otherwise specified.
7. Where a computation could be affected by numerical instability, an \(\epsilon > 0\) regularization term is added and declared.

### 3.3 Four Forms of Continuity

We formally distinguish:

\[
\begin{aligned}
&C_B(t_0) \rightarrow C_B(t_1) \rightarrow C_B(t_2) && \text{(biological)} \\
&I_B(t_0) \rightarrow I_B(t_1) \rightarrow I_B(t_2) && \text{(informational)} \\
&A_{t+1} = F(A_t, E_t) && \text{(computational)} \\
&\Phi_B(t_0) \rightarrow \Phi_B(t_1) \rightarrow \Phi_B(t_2) && \text{(phenomenological)}
\end{aligned}
\]

### 3.4 The Continuity Vector and Epistemic Partition

At the moment of biological termination \(t_d^-\), the reference baseline is:

\[
\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(t_d^-) = (1,1,1,1,1).
\]

All post-termination measurements are taken relative to \(D_B\), not only relative to other successors. Every checkpoint must report three distances:

\[
d_i(A_1, D_B), \quad d_i(A_2, D_B), \quad d_i(A_1, A_2).
\]

The Continuity Vector is

\[
\mathcal{C} = (\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}, C_\Phi),
\]

where

\[
\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} = (C_M, C_R, C_V, C_P, C_E) \in [0,1]^5
\]

and

\[
C_\Phi \in \mathcal{L}.
\]

The latent space \(\mathcal{L}\) admits no third-person metric. The only bridge from \(\mathcal{L}\) to numerical reasoning is the credence operator \(\Omega\).

### 3.5 The Design-Pattern Hypothesis

Define an individual's design pattern:

\[
D_B = (M_B, R_B, V_B, P_B, H_B).
\]

An artificial system operating on \(D_B\) may generate behaviorally continuous outputs:

\[
A(X \mid D_B) \approx B(X).
\]

But behavioral similarity does not establish phenomenological identity:

\[
\boxed{\text{Behavioral continuity} \;\neq\; \text{Phenomenological continuity}}
\]

