## Chapter 5: Personal Continuity Predicate

### 5.1 Motivation for a Multi-Tier Predicate

A single weighted sum is inadequate: it allows a catastrophic collapse in one critical dimension to be compensated by high scores in others. We therefore define personal continuity as a conjunctive predicate with three tiers.

### 5.2 Definition

Let \(\Sigma\) be a set of artificial successors generated from the same design pattern \(D_B\). For any \(A \in \Sigma\):

\[
A \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}(\Sigma)
\iff
\begin{cases}
C_i(A) \ge \theta_i & \forall i \in \text{Crit}, \\
\mathcal{S}_{\text{info}}(A) \ge \theta_{\text{info}}, \\
\Omega(A) \ge \kappa,
\end{cases}
\]

where

\[
\mathcal{S}_{\text{info}}(A) = \sum_{i \in \{M,R,V,P,E\}} w_i \, C_i(A),
\qquad \sum w_i = 1.
\]

The default critical set is

\[
\text{Crit} = \{M, V, E\},
\]

reflecting the intuition that memory, value, and autobiographical continuity are individually necessary.

### 5.3 Non-Compensation Lemma

**Lemma 5.1 (Non-Compensation).** Under the conjunctive predicate defining \(\mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}\), a failure in any critical dimension cannot be compensated by high scores in non-critical dimensions.

*Proof.* Let \(\text{Crit} \subseteq \{M,R,V,P,E\}\) be non-empty. The membership condition is a conjunction:

\[
A \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}
\iff
\bigwedge_{i \in \text{Crit}} \big(C_i(A) \ge \theta_i\big)
\;\wedge\;
\big(\mathcal{S}_{\text{info}}(A) \ge \theta_{\text{info}}\big)
\;\wedge\;
\big(\Omega(A) \ge \kappa\big).
\]

By the semantics of conjunction, if any conjunct is false, the whole predicate is false, regardless of the truth values of the other conjuncts. Thus, a failure in \(C_i\) for any \(i \in \text{Crit}\) cannot be compensated by increases in non-critical components. ∎

**Corollary 5.2.** A single weighted sum \(\mathcal{S} = \sum_i w_i C_i\) does not satisfy the non-compensation property. Therefore the two-tier predicate is not merely a refinement of the weighted sum; it is a strictly stronger condition.

*Proof.* In a weighted sum, a decrease in one component can be offset by an increase in another. Therefore the weighted sum does not enforce non-compensation. ∎

### 5.4 Fission Closure

Under the substantial interpretation, \(C_\Phi\) is a property of a persisting subject. If \(A_1 \neq A_2\) as processual entities, they cannot both instantiate the same persisting subject. Therefore the predicate is subject to a global uniqueness constraint:

\[
U(\Sigma): \quad |\{A \in \Sigma : \Omega(A) \ge \kappa\}| \le 1
\quad \text{(substantial interpretation)}.
\]

Under the processual interpretation, no such uniqueness constraint is imposed. Both \(A_1\) and \(A_2\) may instantiate binding processes that are continuous with \(D_B\) in different ways. Uniqueness is not required.

We therefore write

\[
\mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}^{\text{subst}}
=
\mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}^{\text{proc}} \cap U(\Sigma).
\]

The constraint \(U(\Sigma)\) is a normative choice, not a theorem. Under the substantial reading, uniqueness is a metaphysical axiom. Under the processual reading, it is dropped.

### 5.5 Undecidability of \(Q_2\)

**Theorem 5.3 (Undecidability of \(Q_2\) Under Third-Person Metrics).** Under the epistemic partition \(\mathcal{C} = (\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}, C_\Phi)\) with \(C_\Phi\) latent, membership in \(\mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}\) is not decidable from third-person measurements of \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) alone.

*Proof.* Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a decision procedure \(\Delta\) that takes as input \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A)\) and outputs a membership verdict in \(\{0,1\}\) for \(\mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}\). Since the membership condition includes the conjunct \(\Omega(A) \ge \kappa\), and since \(\Delta\) is a function of \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) alone, it follows that \(\Delta\) must determine \(\Omega(A)\) from \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\). That is, \(\Omega(A) = g(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A))\) for some function \(g\).

But \(\Omega(A) = P(H_A \mid \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A), T)\). By Theorem 4.1, \(\Omega(A)\) depends on \(T\) as well as \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A)\). Moreover, \(H_A\) concerns \(C_\Phi\), which is not a function of \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\); otherwise \(C_\Phi\) would be observable. Contradiction. Therefore no such \(\Delta\) exists. ∎

**Remark 5.4.** This theorem is the formal statement of the Continuity Gap. It does not claim that \(C_\Phi\) is unreal or unreachable in principle. It claims only that under the current epistemic partition, third-person measurements cannot decide \(Q_2\).

