## Chapter 10: Falsification Criteria and Scope

### 10.1 Falsification Criteria

The theory must permit failure. The following observations would weaken or falsify strong versions:

- **F1:** Complete informational replication consistently fails to preserve behavioral identity.
- **F2:** Behavioral continuity is demonstrated while personal identity is independently shown discontinuous.
- **F3:** Consciousness is shown to depend on biological properties not reproducible computationally.
- **F4:** Personal identity requires physical continuity incompatible with substrate transition.
- **F5:** Multiple equally valid artificial successors make unique personal continuity impossible.

### 10.2 Scope Limitation

Experiments 1–3 test \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\). They do not test \(C_\Phi\). Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Experiments 1–3 test } \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} \text{ only. They cannot answer } Q_2.
}
\]

They can test fission, drift, convergence, and collapse of informational continuity. They cannot confirm or falsify \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} \not\Rightarrow C_\Phi\). This limitation is intrinsic to any third-person empirical program under the hard problem. Any figure showing \(\mathcal{D}(X,Y,t)\) must carry this scope limitation as a footnote.

