## Chapter 16: Final Remarks

This dissertation has formalized the Continuity Gap as a structural feature of the relationship between third-person information and first-person experience. It has provided:

1. A precise temporal separation at \(t_d\).
2. An epistemic partition of the Continuity Vector.
3. A Bayesian credence operator \(\Omega(A)\) that respects the latent nature of \(C_\Phi\).
4. A multi-tier personal continuity predicate with non-compensation and fission closure.
5. A Gaussian Process method for baseline construction.
6. A clipped, mean-reverting dynamical model with parameter identification.
7. A distinction between substantial and processual interpretations of \(C_\Phi\).
8. An explicit scope limitation: the empirical program tests \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) only.

The central result is that membership in the personal continuity predicate is undecidable from third-person metrics alone. This is not a defect of the theory; it is the formal statement of the Continuity Gap. \(Q_2\) remains open. The dissertation does not claim to answer it; it formalizes what "answering" would mean.

The next steps are theoretical: formalize \(\Omega_T\) under specific background theories, develop the processual interpretation of \(C_\Phi\), refine the uniqueness constraint \(U(\Sigma)\), and prepare pre-registration artifacts for future empirical work by others. The Continuity Gap is not a problem to be solved by a single theory or experiment. It is a structural feature of the relationship between information and experience. This dissertation provides the tools to reason about it rigorously.

