## Appendix B — Formal Proofs and Derivations

This appendix contains the complete set of proofs for the dissertation. Theorems that have been corrected from earlier versions are marked with a dagger (†). The corrections address the logical gap in the undecidability proof identified in peer review.

### B.1 Non-Compensation Lemma

**Lemma B.1 (Non-Compensation).** Under the conjunctive predicate defining \(\mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}\), a failure in any critical dimension cannot be compensated by high scores in non-critical dimensions.

*Proof.* Let \(\text{Crit} \subseteq \{M,R,V,P,E\}\) be non-empty. The membership condition is a conjunction:

\[
A \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}
\iff
\bigwedge_{i \in \text{Crit}} \big(C_i(A) \ge \theta_i\big)
\;\wedge\;
\big(\mathcal{S}_{\text{info}}(A) \ge \theta_{\text{info}}\big)
\;\wedge\;
\big(\Omega(A) \ge \kappa\big).
\]

By the semantics of conjunction, if any conjunct is false, the whole predicate is false, regardless of the truth values of the other conjuncts. Thus, a failure in \(C_i\) for any \(i \in \text{Crit}\) cannot be compensated by increases in non-critical components. ∎

**Corollary B.2.** A single weighted sum \(\mathcal{S} = \sum_i w_i C_i\) does not satisfy the non-compensation property. Therefore the two-tier predicate is not merely a refinement of the weighted sum; it is a strictly stronger condition.

*Proof.* In a weighted sum, a decrease in one component can be offset by an increase in another. Therefore the weighted sum does not enforce non-compensation. ∎

