### B.2 Undecidability of \(Q_2\) Under Third-Person Metrics†

The v0.6 proof of undecidability assumed that a decision procedure \(\Delta\) must recover \(\Omega(A)\) exactly from \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A)\). This is too strong: \(\Delta\) only needs to decide the boolean \(\mathbb{1}[\Omega(A) \ge \kappa]\). The corrected proof supplies the missing lemma.

**Lemma B.3 (Threshold-Crossing Non-Invariance).** There exists a value of \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) such that for two background theories \(T_1, T_2\),

\[
\Omega_{T_1}(A) \ge \kappa \quad \text{and} \quad \Omega_{T_2}(A) < \kappa.
\]

*Proof.* By the Bayesian definition of \(\Omega\),

\[
\Omega_T(A) = \frac{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} \mid H_A, T) P(H_A \mid T)}{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} \mid T)}.
\]

Consider two theories:

- \(T_1 = T_{\text{func}}\), under which the likelihood ratio is \(> 1\).
- \(T_2 = T_{\text{phys}}\), under which the likelihood ratio is \(\approx 1\) and the prior \(P(H_A \mid T_{\text{phys}})\) is low.

Let \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}^*\) be a value of informational continuity high enough that under \(T_{\text{func}}\), \(\Omega_{T_{\text{func}}}(A) \ge \kappa\). This is possible because the likelihood ratio \(> 1\) amplifies the posterior as \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) increases.

Under \(T_{\text{phys}}\), the posterior odds are

\[
\frac{P(H_A \mid \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}^*, T_{\text{phys}})}{P(\neg H_A \mid \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}^*, T_{\text{phys}})}
=
\frac{P(H_A \mid T_{\text{phys}})}{P(\neg H_A \mid T_{\text{phys}})}
\cdot
\frac{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}^* \mid H_A, T_{\text{phys}})}{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}^* \mid \neg H_A, T_{\text{phys}})}.
\]

Since the prior odds are low and the likelihood ratio is approximately 1, the posterior odds remain low. Therefore \(\Omega_{T_{\text{phys}}}(A) < \kappa\).

Thus, for the same \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}^*\), the threshold-crossing predicate differs across \(T_1\) and \(T_2\). ∎

**Theorem B.4 (Undecidability of \(Q_2\) Under Third-Person Metrics).** Under the epistemic partition \(\mathcal{C} = (\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}, C_\Phi)\) with \(C_\Phi\) latent, membership in \(\mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}\) is not decidable from third-person measurements of \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) alone.

*Proof.* Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a decision procedure \(\Delta\) that takes as input \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A)\) and outputs a membership verdict in \(\{0,1\}\) for \(\mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}\). Since the membership condition includes the conjunct \(\Omega(A) \ge \kappa\), \(\Delta\) must decide the boolean \(\mathbb{1}[\Omega(A) \ge \kappa]\) from \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A)\) alone.

By Lemma B.3, there exists \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}^*\) and theories \(T_1, T_2\) such that \(\Omega_{T_1}(A) \ge \kappa\) and \(\Omega_{T_2}(A) < \kappa\) for the same \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}^*\). A procedure \(\Delta\) that takes only \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) as input cannot distinguish \(T_1\) from \(T_2\), and therefore cannot decide the boolean. Contradiction. Therefore no such \(\Delta\) exists. ∎

**Remark B.5.** This theorem is the formal statement of the Continuity Gap. It does not claim that \(C_\Phi\) is unreal or unreachable in principle. It claims only that under the current epistemic partition, third-person measurements cannot decide \(Q_2\).

**Remark B.6.** The proof does not require that \(\Omega\) vary continuously with \(T\) or that the threshold-crossing be non-invariant for all \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\). It requires only that there exists at least one \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) value for which threshold-crossing differs. This is established by Lemma B.3.

