### B.3 Theory Dependence of \(\Omega\)

**Theorem B.7 (Theory Dependence of \(\Omega\)).** Under different background theories \(T\), the credence \(\Omega_T(A)\) can differ even when \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A)\) is held fixed.

*Proof.* By definition, \(\Omega_T(A) = P(H_A \mid \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A), T)\). Since \(T\) appears explicitly in the conditional, different \(T\) may yield different values. Specifically, the prior \(P(H_A \mid T)\) and the likelihood \(P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A) \mid H_A, T)\) both depend on \(T\). Therefore \(\Omega_T(A)\) is not determined by \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A)\) alone. ∎

**Corollary B.8.** Under physicalism with biological computationalism, \(T_{\text{phys}}\), if

\[
P(H_A \mid T_{\text{phys}}) \text{ is low}
\]

and

\[
\frac{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A) \mid H_A, T_{\text{phys}})}{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A) \mid \neg H_A, T_{\text{phys}})} \approx 1,
\]

then \(\Omega_{\text{phys}}(A)\) remains low regardless of high \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A)\).

*Proof.* The posterior odds are

\[
\frac{P(H_A \mid \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}, T)}{P(\neg H_A \mid \mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}, T)}
=
\frac{P(H_A \mid T)}{P(\neg H_A \mid T)}
\cdot
\frac{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} \mid H_A, T)}{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} \mid \neg H_A, T)}.
\]

If the prior odds are low and the likelihood ratio is approximately 1, the posterior odds remain low. Therefore \(\Omega_{\text{phys}}(A)\) is low. ∎

**Corollary B.9.** Under functionalism, \(T_{\text{func}}\), if the likelihood ratio is \(> 1\), then \(\Omega_{\text{func}}(A)\) can rise with \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A)\).

*Proof.* If

\[
\frac{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} \mid H_A, T_{\text{func}})}{P(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}} \mid \neg H_A, T_{\text{func}})} > 1,
\]

then higher \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) increases the posterior odds, and hence \(\Omega_{\text{func}}(A)\). ∎

**Remark B.10.** Corollaries B.8 and B.9 are conditionals. They specify what follows if the likelihood-ratio conditions hold. The derivation of these conditions from the theories themselves is a task for future theoretical work (OP-11).

**Proposition B.11 (Likelihood-Ratio Assumptions).** Under \(T_{\text{phys}}\) with biological computationalism, the likelihood ratio is approximately 1 because behavioral similarity is expected even without phenomenal continuity. Under \(T_{\text{func}}\), the likelihood ratio is \(>1\) because informational continuity is taken as evidence for phenomenal continuity.

*Justification.* The physicalist-biological computationalist holds that consciousness depends on substrate-specific biological processes. Therefore behavioral similarity between \(B\) and \(A\) does not increase the probability of \(H_A\), because \(H_A\) is already held to be unlikely on biological grounds. The functionalist holds that consciousness depends only on the right pattern of information processing. Therefore behavioral similarity is evidence for \(H_A\), because the pattern is preserved. ∎

**Remark B.12.** Proposition B.11 makes the assumptions explicit. It does not derive them from first principles. This is consistent with the dissertation's methodological stance: the framework formalizes the gap, not the resolution.

