## Appendix L — Supplement on Corrected Theorems

This appendix documents the corrections made to theorems from earlier versions. It is provided for transparency and to facilitate independent verification.

### L.1 Correction to the Undecidability Theorem

**Original statement (v0.6, Theorem 15.2).** Under the epistemic partition \(\mathcal{C} = (\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}, C_\Phi)\) with \(C_\Phi\) latent, membership in \(\mathcal{S}_{\text{personal}}\) is not decidable from third-person measurements of \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) alone.

**Original proof.** Assumed that a decision procedure \(\Delta(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}) \in \{0,1\}\) would have to recover \(\Omega(A) = g(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}(A))\) exactly, and derived a contradiction.

**Defect.** The proof assumed that \(\Delta\) must recover \(\Omega(A)\) exactly. But \(\Delta\) only needs to decide the boolean \(\mathbb{1}[\Omega(A) \ge \kappa]\). These are not the same requirement. It is logically possible for \(\Omega\) to vary with \(T\) while the threshold-crossing happens to be invariant across all \(T\) compatible with a given \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\). The original proof skipped the step of showing that threshold-crossing actually varies.

**Corrected proof.** The corrected proof (Theorem B.4) supplies the missing lemma (Lemma B.3), which establishes that there exists a value of \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) and theories \(T_1, T_2\) such that \(\Omega_{T_1}(A) \ge \kappa\) and \(\Omega_{T_2}(A) < \kappa\). This is sufficient to show that no procedure taking only \(\mathcal{C}_{\text{info}}\) as input can decide the boolean.

**Status.** Corrected in Appendix B.2.

### L.2 Correction to Corollaries 4.2 and 4.3

**Original statement (v0.6).** Under physicalism, if the likelihood ratio is \(\approx 1\), then \(\Omega_{\text{phys}}(A)\) remains low. Under functionalism, if the likelihood ratio is \(> 1\), then \(\Omega_{\text{func}}(A)\) can rise.

**Defect.** These were conditionals with unearned premises. Nothing in the original text derived the likelihood-ratio conditions from physicalism or functionalism themselves.

**Correction.** Proposition B.11 makes the assumptions explicit as justificatory assumptions, not derivations. The derivation of these conditions from the theories themselves is identified as future work (OP-11).

**Status.** Clarified in Appendix B.3.

### L.3 Correction to the GP Baseline

**Original statement (v0.6, Section 6.1).** The GP baseline eliminates recency bias by fitting the pre-mortem trajectory and extrapolating to \(t_d^-\).

**Defect.** The GP baseline does not fully eliminate recency bias; it relocates it into the choice of kernel. For most standard kernels, the posterior mean at the boundary \(t_d^-\) is dominated by the nearest observations. The length-scale hyperparameter does the same job the window \(\tau\) used to do, just less visibly.

**Correction.** Sensitivity analysis across kernel families and length-scales is now required in the same spirit as the weight-vector sensitivity analysis. This is recorded as OP-15.

**Status.** Acknowledged in Appendix I (OP-15) and Chapter 6.

### L.4 Correction to Chapter 15 Duplication

**Original issue (v0.6).** Chapter 15 restated every theorem, corollary, and proof from Chapters 4–9 in full, roughly doubling the page count with nothing new.

**Correction.** The proofs are consolidated in Appendix B as a compiled proof appendix. Chapter 15 is removed as a separate chapter. The compilation is labeled as a reference resource for referees, not as independent content.

**Status.** Resolved by this appendix structure.

